prandtl-number.interpretation:
The Prandtl number ($Pr$) is defined as the ratio of kinematic viscosity ($
u$) to thermal diffusivity ($
u / ho c_p / k$). Here, $
u$ represents the momentum diffusivity, which is a measure of how quickly momentum disturbances propagate through the fluid due to viscous forces. Thermal diffusivity, $\frac{k}{ho c_p}$, represents how quickly heat diffuses through the fluid.  Therefore, $Pr = \frac{
u}{\frac{k}{ho c_p}} = \frac{
u ho c_p}{k}$. A large $Pr$ value (e.g., oils, viscous liquids) implies that momentum diffuses much more readily than heat. This means that velocity gradients in the fluid will be significant relative to temperature gradients. Conversely, a small $Pr$ value (e.g., liquid metals) indicates that heat diffuses much faster than momentum, leading to dominant temperature gradients relative to velocity gradients. Gases typically have $Pr$ values close to 1.
